<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chartres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Autricum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chartres is a city on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Gallo-Roman Autricum, chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>. It later became a seat of the counts of Blois and a centre of learning in the 11th and 12th centuries. Its cathedral, rebuilt after a fire of 1194 with outstanding stained glass, is one of the finest Gothic churches and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chartres.jpg" alt="Chartres" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludvig14 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chartres_DJI_0352a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chartres is built on a hill on the left bank of the River Eure, about 90 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The cathedral stands at the top of the hill, and its two spires can be seen from a long way across the flat country. To the south-east lies the fertile plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauce">Beauce</a>, called the granary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, for which Chartres is the commercial centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chartres was one of the principal towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>, a Celtic tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In the Gallo-Roman period it was called Autricum and afterwards civitas Carnutum, the city of the Carnutes, from which its present name comes. The Norsemen raided and burned it in 858 and besieged it unsuccessfully in 911. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it gave its name to a county held by the counts of Blois and of Champagne and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-chatillon">House of Châtillon</a>, one of whom sold it to the Crown in 1286.</p><p>The English held it from 1417 to 1432 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> raised it to a duchy in 1528. Royal troops of Henry IV took it on 19 April 1591, and on 27 February 1594 Henry was crowned in its cathedral after his conversion to the Catholic faith, the only king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> whose coronation was not held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. In 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> made it a duchy peerage for his nephew Philippe II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, and the title of Duke of Chartres passed to the eldest son of each Duke of Orléans.</p><p>In 1944 Allied bombs destroyed part of the old town, including most of the library of the School of Chartres. The cathedral was spared when Colonel Welborn Barton Griffith Jr. questioned the order to destroy it on 16 August and confirmed that it held no Germans. He was killed later that day, and American divisions liberated the town on 18 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chartres is the prefecture of the Eure-et-Loir department and the seat of a bishopric. Its Gothic cathedral, Notre-Dame de Chartres, is regarded as one of the finest and best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. It replaced a Romanesque cathedral destroyed by fire in 1194, and its stained glass was paid for by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of merchants and craftsmen and by wealthy noblemen. Chartres has been a place of Catholic pilgrimage since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chartres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Rollo’s failed attack on <b>Chartres</b> in 911, King Charles the Simple of West Francia granted him lands around Rouen in return for defending the Seine against other raiders and accepting baptism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gothic architecture began in the Île-de-France with Abbot Suger's rebuilding of the abbey church of Saint-Denis in the 1140s and spread from cathedrals such as <b>Chartres</b>, Reims and Amiens across Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The site of Versailles is first mentioned in 1038 in a charter of the Abbey of Saint-Père de <b>Chartres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune was borrowed from a Catholic French song mocking the Huguenot prince Louis of Condé's failed siege of <b>Chartres</b> in 1568, so a satire of one Protestant prince became a tribute to another.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>A former Protestant who had converted to Catholicism, Henry was crowned at <b>Chartres</b> because Reims was held by the Catholic League.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rollo's Vikings, who had besieged <b>Chartres</b> in West Francia since spring, were defeated when a relief army arrived.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
